---
import FlowDetail from '../FlowDetail.astro';
import FlowDetail from '../../../components/flow/FlowDetail.astro';
import detailResourcePopover from '../../../assets/flow/details/detail-resource-popover.webp';
---
<!-- Chapter 5: The resource readout. Local AI has a cost the cloud hides:
     your own machine does the work, and you hear it. Vocabulary rule from
     the brief: the internal name for this feature never appears in public
     copy; call it what the user sees. The GPU figure is WHOLE-MAC and must
     always say so: per-process GPU attribution does not exist publicly on
     macOS, and claiming Flow's share would be the one thing the platform
     cannot support. Heat is the machine's own verdict, never degrees. -->
<section class="border-t border-border/60 px-6 py-14 sm:py-20" aria-labelledby="tour-resources">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl">
    {/* Apple rhythm at the top: eyebrow, one verbless headline, ONE lede.
        The qualifiers sit beside the picture rather than above it, so the
        reader meets the claim first and the caveats while looking at the
        evidence for them. */}
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl text-center">
      <p class="of-eyebrow" data-animate="rise">What it is using</p>
      <h3 id="tour-resources" class="of-display scroll-mt-28 mt-4 text-[clamp(1.85rem,3.4vw,2.75rem)]" data-animate="lead">
        Answer the whirring fan without opening Activity Monitor.
      </h3>
      <p class="of-lede mx-auto mt-5 text-center" data-animate="rise">
        Processor, memory, graphics, the heat verdict, the model's own footprint, the
        tokens, and the running cost. One place, in plain words, while it works.
      </p>
    </div>
    {/* Re-captured 2026-08-16: the whole popover, not the title-bar strip
        the section used to run. It is the better picture because it is the
        same argument at full strength — this Mac is working hard (graphics
        pinned, a 19.1 GB model resident) and the bill still reads $0.00.
        Portrait, so it sits beside the copy rather than above it. */}
    <div class="mx-auto mt-11 grid max-w-5xl items-center gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.78fr_1.22fr]" data-animate="plate">
      <FlowDetail
        src={detailResourcePopover}
        alt="Flow's What Flow is using popover during a live local run: processor at 35 percent of 14 cores, memory at 53 percent, graphics at 100 percent labelled this whole Mac, Flow itself at 136.9 MB against the muse-glimmer model at 19.1 GB, tokens of 1.3K in and 2.5K out all time, and a spend of zero dollars."
        caption="Graphics is your whole Mac, and says so · Heat is a verdict, not degrees"
        sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 20rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
        glow
      />
      <ul class="flow-list">
        <li>Your own work and the model's are separated. Flow's own footprint sits beside the model it loaded, so you can see which one is actually holding the memory.</li>
        <li>The graphics figure is for your whole Mac, and labels itself that way. Per-process graphics attribution does not exist publicly on macOS, so Flow reports what it can measure instead of inventing a share.</li>
        <li>Tokens count hosted and local runs alike, but only hosted runs cost money. A local run moves thousands of tokens and still bills nothing, because nothing left the machine.</li>
        <li>Heat reads Cool, Warm, Hot, or Too hot. That is the machine's own verdict, not a temperature nobody can act on.</li>
        <li>The model answering names itself at the bottom, so "which model is the fan about" has an answer without leaving your document.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <p class="of-spec-fine mx-auto mt-8 max-w-3xl text-center uppercase tracking-[0.14em]">
      Captured during a live local run, 2026-08-16, on one M3 Max. Session and all-time totals are this machine's own history, not a typical figure.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
